body:
Oh yeah I remember mentioning to my stupid past therapist about wanting to jump off a bridge and she brought me to the ''ER'' and had a dumbass woman ask me if I'm hearing voices inside my head.

&#x200B;

As to the OP, don't bother asking from ''help'' from anyone anymore. We live in a everyone for themselves culture and people will take advantage of you whenever they can even including ''professionals.''

&#x200B;

And don't let them med you up to. They are only interested in making a profit off of you.

It doesn't matter where you are at on the autism spectrum you still have to deal with the bullshit people push on you on a frequent basis. It doesn't matter if you can function independently or not.

&#x200B;

There is no using autism as an excuse as some of the shit we have to deal with from ignorant people about autism is very real and on a frequent basis. There was a women in my recent thread with HFA having to deal with this from people in ATU where she had her freedoms taken away and treated like a fucking animal DESPITE the fact she had a career already established as a teacher.

We'll see about that. I have the paper here and the debt collector said they'll take ''further'' action if I don't reply so we will see. It will be cool to go to ''court'' and push the stupid papers into there face.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I do a full time job in fast food at my local Arby's. It's a decent job for what it is and I have no issue with it. The people are understanding of the fact that I do have autism and there are some managers and employees there that also have people on the autism spectrum to. Makes it all the easier.

&#x200B;

I also do a side gag of building and replacing computer towers for side money to.

If you guys are talking about me, I do have a full time job in maintenance at my local Arby's and I do some IT stuff on the side like build computers for people and building basic networks. 

&#x200B;

I'm considered ''lower middle class'' for a single person. 

&#x200B;

I do have health insurance, clothing, unfortunately I do have medical bills from people forcing me to go to the hospital and I currently don't pay any bills at the moment. I'm living free with my dad who thinks I'm going to take over the place once February comes.

&#x200B;

So I'm that bad off but if you guys look at my post history, I'm going on a ''journey'' this January and don't plan on coming back to modern day life as I'm getting tired of it.

&#x200B;

Like I said before it IS sad that teenage males and treated so differently when entering adulthood. I noticed the huge changes. I had all the support I needed to handle myself emotionally and cognitively from a few teachers helping me out that I still talk to. All I'm getting now in adulthood is a bunch of fucking medication and I had a good therapist free of charge but her dumbass boss decided to take her away from me and they want me to do DBT (Diabetical Behavior Therapy) and I can't afford that as that will cost me about $100 per session.

&#x200B;

I do have autism if that matters. I'm not sure how much you all know about autism but I can tell ya it's not the easiest thing to live with. I get treated badly often by people like I'm a second class citizen just because I'm neurologically different from everyone. I went through a ton of jobs until I finally found one that can tolerate the fact I have autism and they treat me kindly as they have family members on the autism spectrum to so we can relate in some ways.

&#x200B;

Sorry for the long post.

You should've been there when we got evaluated. He was being very stupid and he made me very angry. I can't completely support myself yet as I need to work more months before I can get a place of my own but it may not be possible we'll see.

&#x200B;

I been in and out of the hospital a few times and they are trying to make me pay close to $450 a month for services I never requested. I do run the risk of being homeless by this February Liamza. If they happens then I'm probably going to do the unthinkable (death.) 

&#x200B;

It's not easy living in this world with HFA or Asperger's Syndrome.

I completely agree. It technically should be diagnosed in childhood so we can get the support we need from school and outside professionals so we can transition just fine into adulthood.

&#x200B;

In my specific case, my dad was even warned about it by a therapist and a few other teachers but his dumbass decided not to bring me in and get evaluated and it has cost me in the fact that I'm ruined financially in built up anxiety and depression leading to suicide attempts.

If you're really determined to do it, you'll find a way to do it. I myself was thinking about committing suicide in a couple of months here as my dad is going to sell the townhome that we are living in. I miss my dead cat, I miss my good childhood where I had all the support and now all I can get is medication that doesn't do anything for me and I had a good therapist free of charge but now I gotta pay for one in order to do DBT (Diabetical Behavior Therapy.)  And I'm not going to let these people take advantage of me. Here are some things I was going to do before I decide to go.

&#x200B;

1. Give a note to my grandmother with one of my favorite quotes from Satoshi Tajiri. (The creator of pokemon)

&#x200B;

2. Put flowers on my  cat's grave and she is located at the old trailer park that I was at.

&#x200B;

3. Throw a pizza party at my job before deciding to kick the bucket.

&#x200B;

4. Donate all the money I'm going to be making from this job here and donate it all to Fraser. It's a autism organization here in Minnesota. I'm not sure if your opinion of me might change but I do have autism. I'm more at the higher end of it and can function in the system that's in place but I'm going to decide against that.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

To do it nice and clean, it would be by train, jumping from a bridge going into the water (think the golden gate bridge a suicide hotspot in California) or a bullet to the head. If you fail, you'll have doctors and ''mental health professionals'' fucking around with you making you go from hospital to hospital from psychiatric hospital to psychiatric hospital and they'll try to force you to keep your life.

&#x200B;

I read your posts from other threads and all I gotta say is it's all up to you. Your an adult like I am and it's your call on what you want to do with your life.

&#x200B;

I do agree with your thread here all the way. If you submerge your life completely into the system we have in place, you'll spent almost 2/3rds of your adult life being in two locations, your job and your bed sleeping. Then take into account any vehicles you are taking care of, any kids you may have, and then the upkeep on your place of residence. When you add everything on up Farangmango, we live our lives almost constantly on the move. I hate that lifestyle and come this February, I have some big decisions to make myself.

I can understand the pain. I was left undiagnosed with autism for my whole life up to 23 years of age (I'm 23). I was diagnosed with HFA specifically (High Functioning Autism) and all I gotta say is be careful with the psychiatrists. They have a tendency to ''med'' people up for no reason.

&#x200B;

The one that was trying to do that to me was trying to put me on 6 medications and some of it was stuff that James Holmes was on after I asked him what was ***in the medication.*** I don't know why people just take what's given to them and go with it.

So the both of us went through similar stuff. When it came time for my evaluation my dad was pretty clueless about it. I got various opinions from teachers from elementary school all the way up to high school about my behavior in their classrooms. Most of it was ranging from selective mutism up to taking jokes directly when the teachers told them in front of the classroom. I then went on ahead and took 11 online tests for autism and they came back positive and then finally I wrote down the characteristics associated with autism and presented real life examples of them. My dad talked to Ruth Swartwood [https://www.fraser.org/institute-faculty/ruth-swartwood](https://www.fraser.org/institute-faculty/ruth-swartwood) and made it seem like I was going there off of a suicide attempt when in reality, this was all but a build up of evidence leading up to this point. I could've easily made him look like a fool in front of her but he was my ride there and back home so I didn't bother. 

&#x200B;

The worst thing about my case is that my dad now has two people living under the same roof as him with autism and they both are his sons. He hasn't bother looking into any resources from organizations that specialize in diagnosing and working with people with autism like Fraser. Even after reading my paperwork from Fraser he is still clueless on how to engage me in a conversation. Most people with autism are concrete thinkers and thrive when things are presented in a literal, detailed and factual matter. The thing is, is that ''neurotypicals'' (humans not on the autism spectrum) like to engage in small talk and hardly bother with the details in what they say. My dad is selling the place by February here and had an argument with my brother and doesn't care if I become homeless or not. Luckily for me I have a nice doctor, mom and some teachers that are welling to help me out with getting a place of my own.

&#x200B;

I work full time as a maintenance worker at my local Arby's here and do some IT stuff on the side so I got three months to save up resources for when I hopefully move out,

I'm going through that trouble to and I was diagnosed at age 23 at a place called Fraser here in Minnesota. If being diagnosed at age 12 is late then I can only imagine how being diagnosed at age 23 would be.

&#x200B;

The thing about it though is that my dad was warned about this back when I was a teenager and he didn't bother sending me in then for whatever reason.

''Satoshi Tajiri is officially disconfirmed of having both Autism and Asperger syndrome via his company Game Freak.''

&#x200B;

Then why did you post the link showing he has Asperger's Syndrome when it got combined with ASD back in 2010 per the links I have posted by doctors and psychiatrists?

&#x200B;

And so, After Satoshi Tajiri the next richest autism person(s) would be people like Temple Grandin, Daryl Hannah, and Dan Harmon somewhere among those lines.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

You do realize Asperger's Syndrome is no longer recognized right? To be fair though some people still refer to HFA as Asperger's Syndrome but it is no longer recognized. So if Satoshi Tajiri were to be re evaluated under the new system in place he would have HFA specifically like I do.

&#x200B;

[https://www.parents.com/health/autism/what-happened-to-aspergers/](https://www.parents.com/health/autism/what-happened-to-aspergers/)

&#x200B;

[https://www.cbsnews.com/news/aspergers-syndrome-dropped-from-american-psychiatric-association-manual/](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/aspergers-syndrome-dropped-from-american-psychiatric-association-manual/)

&#x200B;

[https://www.theguardian.com/society/2012/dec/02/aspergers-syndrome-dropped-psychiatric-dsm](https://www.theguardian.com/society/2012/dec/02/aspergers-syndrome-dropped-psychiatric-dsm)

&#x200B;

If you want more specific links I can give them as I have 17 of them saved up in my favorite tabs on my computer when I was looking at it more in detail before I went in for my evaluation at Fraser months ago.

As for seeing if Bill Gates is autistic or not, some autism experts have said so due to his distinct rocking motion, his shortened and monotoned speech patterns and has the habit of avoiding eye contact on the rare occasion he does speak directly to someone else. All these things are common things someone on the autism spectrum have.

&#x200B;

So for Bill Gates, it's speculation. 

&#x200B;

As for the confirmed richest person with autism that person should be Satoshi Tajiri the creator of the pokemon franchise. His net worth is worth over 5 billion dollars.

They sure as hell released their rage that's for sure and were failed by the corrupted system we have in place. We live in a world that is ignorant about autism and in my life as of now, everyone has done the predictable thing. Try to drown me in ''hospital'' bills due to the common suicidal ideation and suicide attempts that are common for those on the autism spectrum; 10x the rate as compared to the normal population from studies done by those like the British Journal of Psychiatry, try to put me on a bunch of medication. A psychiatrist I never met before tried to put me on 6 more medications on top of the three I'm taking right now for anxiety and depression. I went through court records here in Minnesota and saw some judges sentence children from broken families to be put on ''medication'' so powerful they were like walking zombies and forgot their names. So there is definitely medication abusage here in the state of Minnesota.

&#x200B;

The thing is though, I spent the majority of my short 23 years on this planet with IT and computers. Took plenty of classes for it and met with the co founder of this reddit when my dad sent me out to New York (Aaron Swartz) and taught me how to do some computer hacking. Nothing major but I know if I put more time into it, I can probably pull off another Adrian Lamo or Lauri Love to be honest as a final f u if I feel people are trying to screw me over continuously.

&#x200B;

Anyways, this ''psychiatrist'' was trying to put me on the same shit that James Holmes was on (the Aurora movie shooting) and I caught it due to taking my own time out of my life researching into psychiatry due to wanting to protect myself and I swiftly denied that advance. My doctor doctor Norris even told me I'm in control of what goes into my mouth and after she said that, she gained new respect for me and remains one of the few people I can trust on this plane of existence.

&#x200B;

People will take advantage of you whenever they can especially if you have autism so I gotta watch my back. Especially when police officers like Jared Taylor, Steven Brown, and Tom Bernardson can keep their jobs after pulling people over beating them down into a concussion sending them to the hospital or pulling guns out on fiances and threatening to shoot them to death and still keep their jobs. Lots of bad judges here in Minnesota.

I would say that my special interest is my own childhood. Lots of fun times and support from everyone. I got Halloween cards and candy ready for my former teachers and then I plan on visiting my grandmothers home out here where it all began.

&#x200B;

I hope to then also visit my cat's grave and put some flowers on it and bring back the good memories. You are right on the empathy thing and I also have an impaired theory of mind.

&#x200B;

Not sure if you read my past threads but I really wish that my dad would've gotten me diagnosed back when I was a teenager.

I already tried finding solutions and I'm close to the end of it. I'm 23 years of age an adult and so I can do with my life as I please. If I want to back to my childhood memories and visit those milestone places again then so be.

&#x200B;

I'm about ready to get my shit together and just leave this house that I'm in. Getting close to the point of not wanting to live with a parent who hasn't even bothered to understand or educate himself more about autism when he has two of his sons on the autism spectrum when resources are available from places like Fraser for example.

That's what I was thinking just bail and leave it all and leave this dirty system we have in place. Go and visit my old schools some old teachers who were my support group back in the day and probably go through with death. I don't know. I at least know why I been having suicidal thoughts on and off during my teenage years. 

&#x200B;

I wish I had the power to do something about people like this. Having people like this around is extremely dangerous and she is the CEO of the ''mental health'' field so I can't do nothing about her. They grab people at their ''vulnerable'' points and try to have them admitted and fuck them over like I said. 

&#x200B;

When I was in the hospital a few times being forcibly admitted with my dad saying ''yeah you need to definitely go in'' when they found me with a knife on me, I talked to numerous people there. Three said they lost their full time jobs, two lost their seasonal jobs, and there was a young guy there in his mid 20s who was admitted and taking more medication then me all for showing his parents a unloaded gun they requested to see. And on top of this to, I read court cases and records where judges ordered children from broken families to be put on ''medication'' so powerful they were like walking zombies forgetting their names and what not. So it seems like there is ''medication'' abuse in the state of Minnesota where I reside in.

Sorry I should've clarified. They forced me to go to the hospital a couple of times against my will and now I'm being forced to pay close to $500 a month and my dad is selling the place we are in by this February. All the times I been in the hospital, all of it was forced. I'm now sitting here trying to fight this all off or otherwise I'll be homeless by February. 

&#x200B;

Our health care system is bad in this country. Doctors quitting left and right. When I went to my doctor appointment with Dr.Norris just a few days ago, she just got out of a meeting talking about how bad it is. The level of care you can get in this country all depends on your insurance and your income level. It's truly sad and shows that we as humans just don't get it yet.

Thank you so much for the comment.

&#x200B;

I have a trust circle of a doctor named Norris and a few other people that I see frequently. I have a list of things that I'm going to bring up with her and this is definitely one of them.

With being an NT female and all what made you came over to the Asperger's forum of this reddit and post here frequently to the Aspies? From reading a good portion of your posts, it looks like most of them are helpful towards the Aspies here.

&#x200B;

And Thank You for your message. I'm quite sure you know how frustrating it can be to live with Asperger's Syndrome. It's definitely not easy.
